Gavin77 wrote:
> Pet does not always follow you into places
> It is often hard to get your Pet next to the staircase in a dungeon
> Pets often do nothing when they are next to you and you have multiple
> monsters attacking you. They only seem to help sometimes ??
>

In the early game they can be annoying. Sometimes I get lucky and they
follow my character relatively well. Sometimes they annoy the hell out
of me. If I am playing a character that is weak at first, like a
wizard, I try to bear with it and bring them along. Pets are much
easier to manage if you find a magic whistle as those whistles teleport
pets adjacent to you.

> There are too many secrets walls/doors in dungeons. I find it virtually
> impossible to reveal everything on a level without getting hungry. So I
> often miss out on shops etc on levels. You can find and put a ring of
> searching on, but it doesnt always reveal things straight away. And you
> get more hungry if you wear magic rings.
>

I think as you play the game more, they won't be a problem. With some
experience, you can guess where there might be a secret door.

> The biggest problem I have with hunger is that it is increased when you
> are using a magic ring or amulet. Could somebody tell me if wielding a
> magic weapon or if wearing magic armor increases hunger also ??
>

I don't think it goes. It might be easier for you to play a class that
starts out with some food, like a ranger for instance. One strategy I
use for classes who start out with nothing is that I eat the corpses of
newts, orcs, et cetera. There are some you don't want to eat though
like kobolds and such or you'll have a nasty surprise with food
poisoning. A way to gauge this is watch what your pet eats. What your
pet eats is safe for you to eat. From doing this, I can often extend
the time where I will need a food ration and better my odds in finding
rations. I try not to pray for food because I rather save it for when I
am in a tight situation.

> It is way too easy to get your good items corroded. I have discovered
> that bashing acid and ocre jelly with my hands is better. But Gush
> Water traps are common, and there is no way of knowing the trap is
> there ??

You can manual search as you go along if you don't have automatic
searching. This is safer, but time consuming and probably better to
wing it in the early game. I do anyway. In my experience, I don't have
many instances of water traps ruining my armor. Although in this stage
of the game, I am usually wearing armor that I can easily replace.

> I heard you can dip corroded weapons in potions of oil to fix
> them, but I am sure you cannot dip armor ? I heard you can grease your
> items to prevent this, but is it 100% proof against corroding ? Its not
> a good solution anyway, because the grease soon wears off? And any
> weapon you grease gets constantly dropped - not good while in a battle.

I don't know if oil fixes it, but if you read a blessed (I think
blessed, so don't quote me) scroll of enchant weapon/armor while
confused, it fixes the weapon or armor and protects it permanently. To
protect armor, you can read a cursed scroll of destroy armor while
confused. This doesn't repair damage though.



